1
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:13,300
Thus have I heard: At one time, at the Deer Park, the Buddha preached the Four Noble Truths.

2
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:22,400
After that, the following is what Venerable Sariputta taught in detail about the RIGHT MEDITATIVE ENDEAVOUR.

3
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:41,265
My dear fellows, when Right Livelihood is well established and provides a comfortable living, practitioners should begin to carry on the Right Meditative Endeavour, diligently cultivating meditation to attain spiritual awakening.

4
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:49,040
Right Meditative Endeavour is the early stage of meditation practice where results have yet to appear.

5
00:00:49,040 --> 00:01:02,100
Still, practitioners must clearly understand the entire path of cultivation, just as travelers need a clear and detailed map before a long journey to set out with care and confidence.

6
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:11,280
Progress requires unwavering patience and diligence — this is the true meaning of Right Meditative Endeavour.

7
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:18,640
My dear fellows, there are two complementary paths to meditation: Cessation and Insight.

8
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:26,040
Cessation refers to meditation techniques, while Insight refers to contemplation of doctrine.

9
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:33,200
Practitioners should sit in the full lotus posture, as all Buddhas have done to attain enlightenment.

10
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:56,800
The posture should be correct: the spine upright, the body relaxed yet still, the legs firmly crossed, the hands gently resting in the lap, palms facing upward with one atop the other, the arms slightly away from the torso, the eyes gently lowered, the mouth closed, and the tongue lightly touching the upper palate.

11
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:01,231
This is the practice of Body Stabilization.

12
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:14,200
By maintaining correct posture and keeping the body relaxed yet still, practitioners become more clearly aware of the entire body, allowing the mind to grow more mindful.

13
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:27,120
My dear fellows, when practitioners master the practice of Body Stabilization, they should contemplate the obvious truth: The body is impermanent.

14
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:31,000
It will age, fall ill, and eventually die.

15
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,900
After death, it will slowly decay, and even the bones will turn to dust.

16
00:02:36,900 --> 00:02:46,371
As the mind grows more tranquil and alert, they begin to see clearly: This body is breathing in; this body is breathing out.

17
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:54,300
By observing the breath in this way, the mind becomes more tranquil, and wandering thoughts begin to subside.

18
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:58,000
The breath connects Body and Mind, Name and Form.

19
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,750
Whoever can govern the breath gains mastery over the inner soul.

20
00:03:02,750 --> 00:03:06,519
Breathe in, and know that you are breathing in.

21
00:03:06,519 --> 00:03:10,160
Breathe out, and know that you are breathing out.

22
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:20,760
Let this knowing be gentle, without forcing or controlling the breath to be longer, stronger, or deeper than it naturally is.

23
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:31,800
When practitioners observe the breath in this way, they will discover that the source of wandering thoughts lies not in the head but in the lower abdomen.

24
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:39,680
Focusing attention on the head cannot stop the thoughts; on the contrary, it makes the mind tense and tired.

25
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:46,000
There are many other truths that we should gradually contemplate to break false attachments.

26
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:55,040
For instance, practitioners reflect: This world is impermanent, like a dream, full of conflicts and suffering.

27
00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:07,400
My dear fellows, greed, anger, jealousy, arrogance, and irresponsibility are unwholesome states of mind that trap beings in endless suffering and darkness.

28
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:20,000
Simply being aware that the mind is burning with pride, selfishness, greed, anger, jealousy, or irresponsibility will, over time, cause them to fade away.

29
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:31,400
As awareness strengthens, practitioners skillfully guide the breath to become SLOW, LITTLE, AND GENTLE — no longer abundant, heavy, or rapid.

30
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:39,880
As it becomes softer and more subtle, both the body and mind naturally grow still and calm.

31
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:52,850
When practitioners skillfully maintain a slow, little, and gentle breath, there comes a moment when they suddenly find the right volume of breath, where the breath seems to “stick” to the mind.

32
00:04:52,850 --> 00:05:09,120
The breath and mind are bound together, giving practitioners a firm anchor amidst the inner storm — just like someone holding onto a solid pillar amidst a windstorm, remaining steady and unswept.

33
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:19,560
There is a point on the body that the ancient scriptures call Kanda, located about six centimetres below the navel, on the skin's surface.

34
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:29,000
This point is invisible and carries no specific physical sensation; its location can be discerned only through wisdom.

35
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:36,360
By persistently anchoring the mind at Kanda, wandering thoughts gradually gather in one place.

36
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:50,480
It is like gathering scattered debris into a single pile, or tethering a boat, once swept away by wind and waves, firmly to a post on the shore.

37
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:59,400
Practitioners remain aware of arising thoughts but are no longer carried away by them.

38
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:08,760
As the mind rests at Kanda, at first it may still be restless, but it gradually settles into deep stillness.

39
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:12,920
This is the gateway to meditative absorption.

40
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:21,200
In ancient times, when the Buddha was twelve years old, He also anchored His mind at Kanda and entered the First Jhana.

41
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:29,500
The highest technique in meditation is when practitioners find the precise volume of breath where the breath “sticks” to the mind.

42
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:35,624
Once this breath volume is found, just following the breath means controlling the mind.

43
00:06:35,624 --> 00:06:43,200
Do not try to control the mind directly, as doing so may lead to tension or even illness.

44
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:51,560
However, as the mind grows still and joyful feelings arise, practitioners may fall into self-praise and pride.

45
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:56,200
This kind of pride is subtle but dangerous.

46
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:04,500
Dwelling in awareness of the entire body is a technique of Cessation, but it must be paired with Insight.

47
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:14,520
When the awareness of the body becomes clear, they must immediately reflect on the impermanence of the body.

48
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:21,000
The practice of Cessation alone cannot uproot deep-seated ignorance or ego-clinging.

49
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:27,560
Only through Insight meditation can practitioners attain absolute Enlightenment.

50
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:38,600
We must be brave to face the truths of aging, sickness, and death, even while we are still young and well, so that we can let go of trivial worldly desires.

51
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:48,600
As long as we are still alive and healthy, we must treasure our time, doing good deeds and practicing the Dharma diligently.

52
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:52,700
Do not waste this precious human life on meaningless things.

53
00:07:52,700 --> 00:07:58,880
The next truth to contemplate is that the body is unclean by nature.

54
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:09,960
The body should be regarded simply as a tool for doing good and practicing the Dharma, not as an object for pursuing selfish pleasure.

55
00:08:09,960 --> 00:08:14,680
This body is also the result of karma through countless lifetimes.

56
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:21,700
Practitioners must learn to see this body as a mass of sins accumulated from countless past lives.

57
00:08:21,700 --> 00:08:28,700
This body carries a powerful instinctual fetter: the craving for life and the fear of death.

58
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:33,580
In truth, this body is a burden.

59
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:44,900
Even when the mind becomes deeply still and alert, practitioners must contemplate the ultimate truth: this peaceful state is still bound by ignorance and sins.

60
00:08:44,900 --> 00:08:54,160
Unless one attains absolute Enlightenment, with the Three Knowledges and the Six Supernatural Powers, ignorance and sins remain.

61
00:08:54,160 --> 00:09:02,160
Practitioners must engrave this ultimate truth into their hearts until the day of full liberation is reached.

62
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:06,440
The more they progress, the deeper the contemplation must be.

63
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:17,000
Such reflections prevent pride from arising, protect the virtue of humility, and gradually guide practitioners towards complete Non-self.

64
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:29,600
My dear fellows, outside of sitting meditation, in every moment of daily life, practitioners should constantly reflect: “This body is impermanent and sinful.

65
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:33,600
“This self is a mass of ignorance and sins”.

66
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:43,160
Whether awake or asleep, working or resting, do not let even a single moment pass without contemplation.

67
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:49,800
Those who do not yet have the karmic conditions for Enlightenment must walk a more difficult path.

68
00:09:49,800 --> 00:10:01,080
Their spiritual path is full of challenges and requires their practice to be diligent, thorough and precise, even in the smallest details.

69
00:10:01,080 --> 00:10:06,600
My dear fellows, merit is an essential foundation for meditation practice.

70
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:17,400
Even with the right effort and proper technique, without sufficient merit, one still cannot enter deep stillness in meditation.

71
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:35,280
There are two kinds of merit that practitioners must continuously cultivate: first, reverence toward the Perfectly Enlightened Ones, and second, the effort to guide sentient beings in their spiritual practice toward Enlightenment.

72
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:47,800
When the mind grows still, practitioners may feel an urge to let go of everything, which can lead to indifference or a sense of irresponsibility toward other beings.

73
00:10:47,800 --> 00:11:02,120
No matter how still or peaceful the mind becomes, the vow to help all beings must remain unshaken, and the sense of responsibility for all beings must never fade.

74
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:10,600
My dear fellows, only those with great merit can constantly remember: This self is still full of ignorance and sins.

75
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:21,900
Therefore, practitioners must frequently bow in reverence to the Perfectly Enlightened Ones and earnestly repent for unwholesome karma from the past.

76
00:11:21,900 --> 00:11:31,600
When practitioners constantly contemplate “This self is but ignorance and sins”, a deep and sincere humility arises.

77
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:42,160
They then no longer desire lofty spiritual attainments but merely wish to always remember this truth for the rest of their lives.

78
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:46,098
We bow in deep reverence to the Compassionate Buddha.

79
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:57,400
May the Buddha bless us to forever remember the sacred words of the Sutra of Right Meditative Endeavour, a noble truth proclaimed by all Buddhas.

80
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:11,500
We bow in deep gratitude to Venerable Sariputta, the One foremost in wisdom, who compassionately explained these profound teachings, guiding us steadily on the path of liberation.

81
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:29,640
May all sentient beings develop unshakable determination, steadily accumulate infinite merit, diligently practice meditation in the right way, and forever follow the Buddha toward the entire Enlightenment.

82
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:36,533
NAMO THE FOREMOST WISDOM VENERABLE SARIPUTTA, LEADER OF THE SANGHA.

83
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:42,320
NAMO SHAKYA MUNI BUDDHA.

84
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:46,360
NAMO SHAKYA MUNI BUDDHA.

85
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:50,440
NAMO SHAKYA MUNI BUDDHA.
